-\reviewcomment{2.3} \textbf{Limited novelty.} The paper positions AQT as a key contribution, but AQT largely resembles an existing SQL AST or intermediate representation augmented with abstract labels, read/write sets, dependency edges, and schema context. The authors do not clearly articulate what fundamentally new capability AQT provides over existing SQL representations (e.g., JOOQ's parse tree or SQLGlot's AST) or translation systems (CrackSQL, RISE, QTRAN). The distinction from these systems is insufficiently developed.
+\reviewcomment{2.3} \textbf{Contribution of AQT and compositional baselines.} The authors do not clearly articulate what fundamentally new capability AQT provides over existing SQL representations (e.g., JOOQ's parse tree or SQLGlot's AST) or translation systems (CrackSQL, RISE, QTRAN). More importantly, the evaluation lacks compositional baselines such as ``existing SQL translator + SEDAR/GRIFFIN'' or ``AST-based translation + GRIFFIN''.
 
-    More importantly, the evaluation lacks compositional baselines such as ``existing SQL translator + SEDAR/GRIFFIN'' or ``AST-based translation + GRIFFIN''. Without such ablations, it is difficult to determine whether the contribution is a fundamentally new transfer technique or mainly an engineering integration of existing components. The authors should add such baselines or provide a clearer theoretical argument for why AQT enables capabilities that prior representations cannot achieve.
+\reply
+We have clarified what the AQT contributes beyond existing SQL representations and translation systems in Sections~II, III, and VI. Details can be found in \metareviewref{2}.
 
